Knowledge-intensive reasoning systems increasingly rely on reflective steps such as self-evaluation, error diagnosis, and plan revision to organize verification workflows. This paper presents Structurally-Calibrated Functional Attribution (SC-FMA), a methodology for auditable verification-step weighting in knowledge-intensive reasoning systems. SC-FMA converts local functional utility signals into calibrated supervision weights through the Structurally-Calibrated Utility (SCU) objective, a convex optimization problem that combines fidelity to utility, graph-level structural necessity, redundancy reduction, and bottleneck protection. On a controlled synthetic benchmark with proxy step-importance labels, the full Quadratic Program (QP) variant is strongest ($\rho=0.608$) and improves over raw CIU ($\rho=0.483$). On PRM800K real process-supervision annotations, the primary positive evidence is instead the stored \texttt{w\_struct} step-ranking signal ($\rho=0.611$); Ridge is the closest SC-FMA approximation ($\rho=0.604$), while QP ($\rho=0.442$) and Projection ($\rho=-0.135$) are downgraded for this real-data route. A new PRM800K audit-prioritization readout shows that the same locked-split evidence can concentrate high-rated process steps under a fixed review budget, but only as offline audit context. GSM8K/HotpotQA task-specific replay, downstream filtering, PRM training, and out-of-distribution transfer remain unsupported. The contribution is therefore a claim-bounded calibration methodology for KBS-oriented verification-step weighting, not a deployed training or replay validation result.
